        "title": "05 - Hot Metal: Mechanical Type Composition (1884–1970s)",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Hot metal typesetting was the industrial solution to a 400-year-old bottleneck: hand composition was still being done character-by-character in 1884, exactly as Gutenberg's compositors had done it. The Linotype and Monotype machines solved this at the cost of architectural complexity that makes them engineering marvels — distributed manufacturing systems operated from a typewriter keyboard."
